, C, D. Let's break them down.\n\nOption A says that using multimedia courseware has obvious advantages over traditional blackboard writing. The context does suggest that multimedia can improve effects, but it doesn't explicitly state that it's always better. It just says it can improve them, but maybe it's not always the case. So A might not be the hypothesis.\n\nOption B states that transferring content from blackboard writing to courseware won't reduce the effectiveness of blackboard writing. That makes sense because the context mentions that even if the courseware is simple, the blackboard writing can still be effective. So B seems like a possible hypothesis.\n\nOption C claims that some teachers' courseware is too simple to fully utilize multimedia. The context does mention that simple courseware can still use the effect of multimedia, but it's more about the effectiveness rather than the teachers' courseware being too simple. So C might not be the main hypothesis.\n\nOption D suggests that replacing blackboard writing with multimedia saves time. The context does say that blackboard writing can save time, but it's more about the effectiveness of the teaching method rather than saving time. So D might not be the hypothesis.\n\nPutting it all together, the argument is that while multimedia can improve teaching, it's not necessary because blackboard writing can still be effective and save time. So the hypothesis is that transferring content to courseware won't reduce the effectiveness of blackboard writing. Therefore, the option is "]]
