 be on, and if X is on, T can't be on. Same for their off states.\n\n4. If Y and Z are turned on at the same time, then W must also be turned on. So, Y ∧ Z → W. That means whenever both Y and Z are on, W has to be on. But if either Y or Z is off, W can be either on or off.\n\nNow, the question states that both S and Z are turned on. So, S = on, Z = on.\n\nLet's see what this implies.\n\nFrom condition 2: Since S is on, T must be on. Because only when S is off can T be off. So, if S is on, T can't be off, so T must be on.\n\nFrom condition 3: Since T is on, X must be off. Because T and X can't be on at the same time. So, X = off.\n\nFrom condition 1: If W is on, then X must be on. But we just determined that X is off. So, if X is off, then W can't be on. Because if W were on, X would have to be on, which it isn't. Therefore, W must be off.\n\nFrom condition 4: Since Z is on, if Y is also on, then W must be on. But we just determined that W is off. So, if W is off, then Y can't be on at the same time as Z. Because if Y and Z were both on, W would have to be on, which it isn't. Therefore, Y must be off.\n\nSo, let's summarize what we have so far:.\n\n- S = on.\n- Z = on.\n- T = on (because S is on).\n- X = off (because T is on).\n- W = off (because X is off).\n- Y = off (because if Y were on, W would have to be on, which it isn't).\n\nNow, let's look at the options:.\n\nA) T is turned on and Y is turned off. Well, T is on and Y is off, so this seems to fit. But let's check the others.\n\nB) Both T and Y are turned off. But we have T on and Y off, so this isn't correct.\n\nC) Both W and T are turned on. But W is off and T is on, so this is wrong.\n\nD) X is turned on and Y is turned off. X is off, so this is incorrect. Therefore, the option is "]]
