 they don't want to stay there, but the data shows they do. Hmm, this is a bit confusing. Maybe I'm misunderstanding. Let me think again. If it's harder in big cities, people might prefer to go back home where it's easier. But the data shows they're staying in big cities, so this option doesn't explain that. Wait, no, the option says it's more difficult in big cities, so maybe they don't want to stay there, but the data shows they do. So this option doesn't account for the phenomenon, making it a possible answer. But I'm not sure yet.\n\nOption D: The infrastructure of big cities is better and more convenient than small and medium-sized cities. If big cities have better infrastructure, that makes them more attractive places to live and work. So, people might choose to stay there rather than go back home. This would explain why a large proportion are returning to big cities instead of going back home. So, this option does account for the phenomenon, meaning it's not the answer we're looking for.\n\nWait, so I'm a bit confused between options B and C. Let me clarify.\n\nThe question is about why a large proportion of people who originally fled from other cities to go back home are now returning to big cities instead. So, the phenomenon is that many are staying in big cities rather than going back home.\n\nOption B says that most people returning from big cities to small cities are not fit for work and life. But the context is about people going back home, not returning to small cities. So, this option is talking about the opposite situation and doesn't explain the phenomenon. Therefore, it doesn't account for why people are returning to big cities, making it the correct answer.\n\nOption C says it's harder in big cities, so people might prefer small cities. But the data shows they're staying in big cities, so this doesn't explain the phenomenon. However, I'm not sure if this is the best answer because the option is about difficulties, which might not directly relate to the decision to stay in big cities.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which option does NOT account for the phenomenon. So, the correct answer is the one that doesn't explain why people are returning to big cities.\n\nOption B is about people returning to small cities, which is the opposite of the context. So, it doesn't explain the phenomenon at all. Therefore, the option is "]] 10%|#         | 62/600 [01:24<08:13,  1.09it/s]
