le before the experiment. This seems important because if the starting point was the same, it makes the results more reliable. If one group had naturally more wrinkle-prone skin, that could have skewed the results. So this supports the conclusion by ensuring that the only difference was the aspirin intake.\n\nB) The number of subjects in the two groups is equal. Equal numbers are good for experiments because it helps ensure that the results aren't skewed by one group being larger. But does this directly support the conclusion about aspirin's effectiveness? It's more about the validity of the experiment rather than the effectiveness itself.\n\nC) The first group took a larger amount of aspirin. This is a bit tricky. The context says the first group took aspirin for a long time, but it doesn't specify the dosage. If they took a larger amount, it might suggest more effectiveness, but the context doesn't mention dosage. So this might not be necessary for supporting the conclusion.\n\nD) Aspirin is artificially synthesized. Well, aspirin is a known chemical compound, so this is true, but how does it support the conclusion about wrinkle prevention? It doesn't directly relate to the experiment's results. Therefore, the option is "]]
