ommendation." But the context doesn\'t specify whether they completely stop hearing praise or just start ignoring it. So maybe D is a bit more accurate because it captures the idea that they eventually stop believing in the praise.\n\nBut let me think again. The context says that such children never hear any praise at all, which is a stronger statement than just discounting all words. So maybe D is not exactly matching the context because the context implies that they stop believing in any praise, not necessarily that they stop hearing it. Hmm, this is a bit confusing.\n\nWait, the context says that if parents uniformly praise their attempts, regardless of success or failure, the children will eventually discount all words of commendation. So the conclusion is about the effect on the children\'s self-esteem, which is suffering because they don\'t receive genuine positive feedback. So B is directly stating that the self-esteem suffers when parents uniformly praise attempts, which matches the context.\n\nOption D is about children learning to discount all praise, which is a consequence, but the question is about the overall conclusion. The overall conclusion is about the negative effect on self-esteem, so B is more about the outcome than the process. Therefore, the option is ']] 16%|#6        | 98/600 [01:54<06:56,  1.21it/s]
