s talks about cost. If it's expensive, maybe people won't replace their drives, but does that directly question his point? Maybe not directly, but it's a factor in the feasibility.\n\nB) The profit of selling solid state drives is much higher than traditional ones. This is about business, not about the performance issue. It doesn't directly relate to why laptops run slowly.\n\nC) A small number of old laptops have poor CPU performance and small memory capacity. Wait, Mr. Tian said the main issue isn't CPU or memory, so if a small number have poor CPU and memory, that might mean that for most people, the main issue is the hard drive. But this seems to support his point, not question it. So maybe not the right answer.\n\nD) The game experience depends more on the graphics card than the hard disk, and old laptops have poor graphics. Oh, this is interesting. If the graphics card is the main factor in game experience, then the hard drive's speed isn't as crucial. So, even if you upgrade the hard drive, if the graphics are bad, the gaming experience won't improve much. That directly challenges Mr. Tian's point because he focused on the hard drive, but the real issue is the graphics card. Therefore, the option is "]]
