he context first. It's saying that for a leader to be effective, two things must be true: the followers must believe their efforts are necessary for the goal, and that those efforts will actually lead to the goal's achievement. So both beliefs are necessary for following the leader.\n\nNow, looking at the options:.\n\nOption A: Some leaders who convince their followers of the necessity of their efforts still fail to lead them to the goal. Hmm, this seems possible because even if the followers believe their efforts are necessary, maybe there are other factors, like the leader's charisma or the team's dynamics, that might prevent them from achieving the goal. So this doesn't contradict the context.\n\nOption B: Sometimes people who are unsure about whether their efforts are needed still follow a leader. Wait, the context says that followers are convinced both that their efforts are necessary and that they'll achieve the goal. If they're unsure about the necessity, that means they don't believe their efforts are needed, which according to the context, should prevent them from following. So this seems contradictory because the context says followers only follow if they believe both things. So this might be the answer.\n\nOption C: Only if one is a leader must one convince people of the necessity of their efforts. The context says that leaders must convince followers, but it doesn't specify that only leaders can do this. So this statement is too restrictive and doesn't necessarily contradict the context. It might be a trick option.\n\nOption D: Conveying the relationship between efforts and the goal leads to following the leader. This aligns with the context because the leader successfully communicates the necessary relationship, so the followers will follow. So this is consistent and not the answer. Therefore, the option is "]]
