el Prize doesn't necessarily make people live longer, or maybe that the Nobel Prize itself isn't the cause of their longer lifespans.\n\nLet me look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: The Nobel Prize in Economics is only awarded to living scholars, and this rule is beneficial to those who have lived a long life. Hmm, this seems to support the expert's claim. If the award is given only to living people, it might attract scholars who are already living longer, so their longevity could be a result of being eligible for the prize, not the prize itself. So this actually strengthens the expert's argument, not weakens it. So A is probably not the right answer.\n\nOption B: Winning the Nobel Prize can bring great physical and mental pleasure, and a happy state can prolong life. This seems to suggest that the happiness from winning the prize might be what's causing the longer lifespan, not the prize itself. So this weakens the expert's conclusion because it introduces another factor (happiness) as the reason for longer life. So B could be a candidate.\n\nOption C: Keynes, who is the father of macroeconomics, died at 63 and didn't win the Nobel Prize in Economics. This is interesting because it shows that not everyone who could have won the prize did, and some winners might not have been eligible. However, this doesn't directly address whether winning the prize causes longer life. It just provides a data point that some Nobel laureates didn't live long. So C might weaken the argument a bit, but not as much as B.\n\nOption D: Scholars who won the Nobel Prize in Physics have long lifespans, but none in Economics. Wait, this is a bit confusing. If physics laureates live longer, but none in Economics have won it, does that mean that Economics laureates don't live longer? But the expert's point is that Economics laureates do live longer. So if D is true, it actually supports the expert's claim because it shows that Physics laureates do live longer, but Economics laureates don