rking conditions. The question is asking which conclusion is most likely drawn from this passage.\n\nNow, looking at the options:.\n\nA) A marriage based purely on beauty and passion will be a failure and an unfortunate one.\n- The context doesn't talk about beauty or passion being the main factors in marriage. It's more about economic security and material comforts. So I don't think A is the right answer.\n\nB) Affection is the most important factor in a marriage, not financial security or material comforts.\n- The context actually suggests the opposite. It points out that while some marriages are based on economic factors, the trend is decreasing. It also mentions that parents still use family, education, and work as references, implying that financial factors are still significant. So B doesn't seem right either.\n\nC) Women in modern society need to enhance their status through wealth and status in marriage.\n- The context doesn't specifically address the roles of men and women in marriages. It talks more about general factors like economic security and parental influence. So I'm not sure about this one.\n\nD) In order to have a good marriage, we should have a correct view of marriage and attitude towards marriage.\n- The context seems to suggest that while economic factors are influencing marriages, they aren't the only or most important factors. It also implies that parents are still using various criteria, which might indicate a need for a balanced view of marriage beyond just material comforts. So D seems plausible.\n\nWait, but the context doesn't explicitly talk about a correct view of marriage, just that parents are using certain factors. Maybe D is about understanding that marriage isn't just about money but also about other aspects like affection and personal values. So D might be the intended conclusion.\n\nI think the key point is that the passage is highlighting that money is still a significant factor, so the conclusion should reflect that a correct