sis issue.\n\nOption B: More male rats have autism than female. This is about gender differences, which could be a factor, but again, it's not directly related to the protein synthesis aspect. It's more of a demographic observation.\n\nOption C: Inhibiting neuronectin synthesis relieves autism symptoms. This seems relevant because if less synthesis leads to fewer symptoms, it supports the idea that too much synthesis is problematic. This directly ties the protein synthesis to autism, which is exactly what the context is about.\n\nOption D: Removing genes that control synthesis increases neuronectin. Wait, if removing genes (which control synthesis) leads to more neuronectin, that's the opposite of what the context says. The context says too much neuronectin causes autism, so this option suggests that less synthesis (from removing genes) would be better, but here it's increasing, which doesn't support the view. In fact, it contradicts it. Therefore, the option is "]]
