ct if A is in position 1, which is possible.\n\nNow, let's see the adjacent pairs in Qian Liang's prediction. The adjacent pairs are A-E, E-B, B-C, C-D. Qian Liang guessed the correct order of two of these pairs.\n\nSo, in the actual ranking, two of these pairs must be in the correct order as per Qian Liang's prediction.\n\nLet me try to construct the actual ranking step by step.\n\nFirst, from Zhao Ming's prediction, E is not first, D is not second, C is not third, A is not fourth, B is not fifth.\n\nSo, possible positions:.\n\n1: A, B, C, D (but not E).\n2: A, B, C, E (but not D).\n3: A, B, D, E (but not C).\n4: B, C, D, E (but not A).\n5: A, C, D, E (but not B).\n\nWait, that's a bit confusing. Let me try another approach.\n\nLet me list the constraints again:.\n\n1. Actual ranking ≠ Zhao Ming's prediction: E, D, C, A, B.\n   - So, E ≠ 1, D ≠ 2, C ≠ 3, A ≠ 4, B ≠ 5.\n\n2. Actual ranking has two positions correct as per Qian Liang's prediction: A, E, B, C, D.\n\n3. Actual ranking has two adjacent pairs correct as per Qian Liang's prediction.\n\nLet me consider possible correct positions in Qian Liang's prediction.\n\nCase 1: Suppose A is in position 1 (correct). Then, from Zhao Ming's prediction, A is not in position 4, so that's fine.\n\nNow, let's see the adjacent pairs. Qian Liang's prediction has A-E as the first pair. So, if A is in position 1, then E must be in position 2 for that pair to be correct. But from Zhao Ming's prediction, E is not in position 2, so E cannot be in position 2. Therefore, the A-E pair cannot be correct. So, if A is in position 1, the A-E pair is incorrect.\n\nSo, the correct adjacent pairs must be among E-B, B-C, C-D.\n\nNow, let's see if any of these can be correct.\n\nIf E is in position 2, but from Zhao Ming's prediction, E is not in position 2, so E is not in position 2. So, E cannot be in position 2, meaning the E-B pair cannot be correct.\n\nSimilarly, if B is in position 3, then the B-C pair would be correct if C is in position 4. But from Zhao Ming's prediction, C is not in position 3, so C could be in position 3 or elsewhere.\n\nWait, this is getting complicated. Maybe I should try each option and see which one fits.\n\nThe options are:.\n\nA) A, B, C, D, E.\nB) B, A, E, C, D.\nC) E, D, A, B, C.\nD) C, D, E, A, B.\n\nLet me check each option against the constraints.\n\nOption A: A, B, C, D, E.\n\nCheck Zhao Ming's prediction: E, D, C, A, B. None of the positions match, so that's good.\n\nCheck Qian Liang's prediction: A, E, B, C, D.\n\nIn the actual ranking, A is in position 1 (correct), E is in position 3 (incorrect, as Qian Liang predicted E in 2), B is in position 4 (correct), C is in position 5 (incorrect), D is in position 3 (incorrect). So, Qian Liang has two correct positions: A in 1 and B in 4. That's good.\n\nNow, check the adjacent pairs in Qian Liang's prediction:.\n\nA-E: A is 1, E is 3. So, in actual ranking, A is 1, E is 3. So, the order is A, B, E. So, the pair A-E is not adjacent, so this pair is incorrect.\n\nE-B: E is 3, B is 4. In actual ranking, E is 3, B is 4. So, E-B is adjacent and in the correct order. So, that's one correct adjacent pair.\n\nB-C: B is 4, C is 5. In actual ranking, B is 4, C is 5. So, B-C is adjacent and correct. That's another correct adjacent pair.\n\nC-D: C is 5, D is nowhere. Wait, D is in position 5 in the actual ranking? No, in option A, D is in position 4. Wait, no, in option A, the ranking is A, B, C, D, E. So, D is in position 4, E in 5.\n\nWait, Qian Liang's prediction has C-D as the last pair. In actual ranking, C is 3, D is 4. So, C-D is adjacent and correct. So, that's three correct adjacent pairs, but Qian Liang only guessed two. So, this option has three correct adjacent pairs, which is more than allowed. Therefore, option A is invalid.\n\nOption B: B, A, E, C, D.\n\nCheck Zhao Ming's prediction: E, D, C, A, B. None of the positions match. E is not 1, D is not 2, C is not 3, A is not 4, B is not 5. So, that's good.\n\nCheck Qian Liang's prediction: A, E, B, C, D.\n\nIn actual ranking, positions:.\n\n1: B (incorrect).\n2: A (incorrect, Qian Liang predicted A in 1).\n3: E (incorrect, Qian Liang predicted E in 2).\n4: C (incorrect, Qian Liang predicted C in 4).\n5: D (incorrect, Qian Liang predicted D in 5).\n\nWait, none of the positions are correct. But Qian Liang should have two correct positions. So, this option is invalid.\n\nOption C: E, D, A, B, C.\n\nCheck Zhao Ming's prediction: E, D, C, A, B.\n\nIn actual ranking, E is 1 (correct), D is 2 (correct), A is 3 (incorrect, Zhao Ming's prediction has A in 4), B is 4 (incorrect, Zhao Ming's prediction has B in 5), C is 5 (incorrect, Zhao Ming's prediction has C in 3). So, Zhao Ming has two correct positions, which contradicts the condition that he has none correct. Therefore, option C is invalid.\n\nOption D: C, D, E, A, B.\n\nCheck Zhao Ming's prediction: E, D, C, A, B.\n\nIn actual ranking:.\n\n1: C (incorrect, Zhao Ming's prediction has E in 1).\n2: D (incorrect, Zhao Ming's prediction has D in 2, but wait, D is in 2 in actual ranking, but Zhao Ming's prediction has D in 2. Wait, no, Zhao Ming's prediction is E, D, C, A, B. So, position 2 is D, which is correct in actual ranking. But the condition is that Zhao Ming didn't guess any correct positions. So, this is invalid because D is in position 2 in actual ranking, which matches Zhao Ming's prediction. Therefore, option D is invalid.\n\nWait, that's a problem. So, none of the options seem to fit. Did I make a mistake?.\n\nWait, let me re-examine option A.\n\nOption A: A, B, C, D, E.\n\nQian Liang's prediction: A, E, B, C, D.\n\nIn actual ranking, A is 1 (correct), E is 3 (incorrect), B is 4 (correct), C is 5 (incorrect), D is nowhere. Wait, D is in position 4 in actual ranking, but Qian Liang's prediction has D in 5. So, D is in 4, which is incorrect.\n\nNow, adjacent pairs:.\n\nQian Liang's prediction: A-E, E-B, B-C, C-D.\n\nIn actual ranking:.\n\nA is 1, B is 2: A-B (incorrect, Qian Lia