ld also have d.\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nOption A talks about Tao yuanming and Li Bai not doing certain things, and concludes that ambitious people aren't proud. This seems more like a deductive argument or maybe an analogy based on personal examples, but I'm not sure if it's strictly based on attribute similarity.\n\nOption B compares light and sound, noting they both have linear propagation, refraction, and reflection, and then concludes light also has fluctuation. This seems like an analogy because it's using the shared properties (a, b, c) to infer another property (d) about light.\n\nOption C contrasts Bethune with irresponsible people and concludes he has a great spirit. This feels more like a categorical statement rather than an analogy because it's comparing Bethune to a group rather than another object with similar attributes.\n\nOption D compares silver and copper in terms of electrical conductivity and resistivity. It's a straightforward comparison of two materials, using their shared attributes (metals, conductivity) to conclude about resistivity. This seems more like a scientific argument than an analogical one because it's not inferring another attribute through similarity but rather stating a fact about their properties.\n\nSo, breaking it down, the key is to find the option where two things share some attributes and then one has an additional attribute that the other is inferred to have. Therefore, the option is "]]
