ect link to sugar sales dropping. It might not weaken the conclusion because it's a different product.\n\nB) Today, the average consumption of white sugar per person is 80% of what it was 10 years ago. Okay, this is about consumption, not sales. If people are eating less sugar on average, that could explain the drop in sales. So this option suggests that the decrease is because people are using less sugar, not necessarily because they stopped buying it. That would weaken the argument that the drop is due to public vigilance.\n\nC) After reducing the intake of white sugar, some sweet lovers have sleep disorders. This seems to link sugar consumption to health issues, specifically sleep problems. It supports the idea that people are cutting back because of health concerns, which actually strengthens the original conclusion. So this wouldn't weaken the argument; it would make it stronger.\n\nD) In recent years, the price of white sugar has soared due to a sharp reduction in the area of sugarcane planting. High prices could lead to people buying less, but this is an economic factor. If the price went up, people might buy less, but the original conclusion is about public vigilance. This is a possible alternative explanation, but I'm not sure if it's as strong as option B. It's more about economic reasons rather than health concerns.\n\nSo, comparing B and D, B directly points to a decrease in consumption due to lower intake, which is a different reason than public health vigilance. D is about price affecting sales, which is another factor but might not be as directly tied to the health argument as B is. Therefore, the option is "]]
