ption is suggesting that the title should be more specific, maybe limiting it to that particular group. But does this affect the reliability of the statistical inference? I don't think so. The title is just a summary; it doesn't question the data itself. It's more about how the information is presented rather than the data's validity.\n\nOption B: When the paternity test is conducted, there are already doubts about the paternity relationship. This seems like a valid point. If there are already doubts about the paternity when the test is done, that could introduce bias or uncertainty into the results. For example, if the test is being done on cases where paternity isn't clear, the 15% figure might not accurately reflect the true paternity rates. So this would question the reliability.\n\nOption C: Can modern science and technology really accurately identify paternity? This is a critical question. If the technology isn't accurate, then the test results themselves might be flawed. If the tests aren't reliable, then the 15% figure could be misleading. So this would question the reliability of the statistical inference.\n\nOption D: The cost of paternity testing is too high. High costs could affect participation rates. Maybe only wealthier individuals can afford the tests, which might skew the sample and make the results less representative of the general population. So this could question the reliability of the statistics.\n\nPutting it all together, the question is asking which option does NOT question the reliability. Option A is about the title, which doesn't affect the data's validity. The other options (B, C, D) all point to potential issues that could affect how the statistics are interpreted or the accuracy of the data. Therefore, the option is "]]
