even though more books are read, the average per child is lower. Or perhaps the increase in reading is not reflected in the average because the data is normalized differently.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the increase in the percentage of families reading doesn\'t necessarily mean that each child is reading more. Maybe some children are reading a lot more, while others aren\'t, but the overall average is still lower.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which option best explains the phenomenon. The phenomenon is that despite more parents reading with their children, the average number of books read per child decreased.\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) Children have heavy schoolwork and don\'t have time for extracurricular reading.\n\nB) Parents born in the 1980s and 1990s are highly educated and value reading.\n\nC) Children are used to listening to books in parent-child reading, which isn\'t counted in 2018.\n\nD) Electronic reading on phones and computers isn\'t considered in children\'s books.\n\nSo, the phenomenon is that more parents are reading with their children, but children are reading fewer books on average. So, why would more reading happen but the average go down?.\n\nOption A suggests that children have too much schoolwork and don\'t have time for extracurricular reading. That could explain why despite more parents reading, children aren\'t reading as much because they\'re too busy with schoolwork. So, the increase in reading by parents might not translate to more reading by children because the children are too occupied.\n\nOption B says parents born in the 1980s and 90s are highly educated and value reading. That might explain why reading is more common, but it doesn\'t directly explain why the average books per child went down. It might support the increase in reading, but not the decrease in average.\n\nOption C suggests that children are used to listening to books during parent-child reading, which isn\'t counted. If listening to books is considered