ch as the total reading (including listening).\n\nAlternatively, Option A suggests that schoolwork is taking away time, so children aren\'t reading more despite more parents reading with them.\n\nI think both A and C could be possible, but I need to see which one better explains the phenomenon.\n\nThe phenomenon is that the average books per child went down, even though more parents were reading with them. So, if the children are too busy with schoolwork (A), they have less time to read. If the data doesn\'t count listening sessions (C), then the actual reading by children didn\'t increase.\n\nBut the question is about why the average went down. If the data includes both reading and listening, but the average is based on books read by the child, then if parents are reading more, but children aren\'t reading more, the average would go down. So, maybe the issue is that the children aren\'t reading as much as they used to, perhaps because they\'re too busy with schoolwork or because they\'re not getting to read physical books.\n\nBut the context says that the amount of books read by parents and children increased. So, if the data includes both reading and listening, then the total books read by parents and children went up, but the books read by the children themselves (which is what the average is based on) didn\'t increase as much, leading to a lower average.\n\nWait, but the context says the amount of books read by parents and children increased by 1.8 percentage points. So, if the data is about the total books read by both, including both parents and children, then the average per child would be higher if the total went up. But the average went down, so that doesn\'t make sense.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m misunderstanding. Let me think again.\n\nThe context says: "the amount of books read by parents and children increased by 1.8 percentage points compared to 2017 and the length of books read also increased compared to last year. But in 2018, the city\'s children read just