nings and deception in habit breaking.\n\nNow, looking at the options:.\n\nA) The more the relevant danger is exaggerated, the more likely one is to break one's habit.\n\nThis seems to suggest that exaggeration leads to success. But the argument doesn't explicitly state this as an assumption. It's more about the necessity of external warnings rather than the effect of exaggeration itself.\n\nB) People generally do not find it easy to deceive themselves.\n\nThis seems relevant because the argument mentions that without external warnings, people can't easily deceive themselves. So this might be an assumption because the argument relies on the idea that self-deception is difficult, making external warnings necessary.\n\nC) A doctor is justified in deceiving a patient whenever doing so is likely to make the patient healthier.\n\nThis is about the justification of a doctor's actions. The argument doesn't directly address whether doctors are justified or not, just that they provide warnings. So this might not be the assumption.\n\nD) Most of the techniques that help people quit smoking can also help people break other habits.\n\nThe argument says that the strategy of exaggerating dangers can be used for other habits, so this seems like a point the argument makes, not an assumption. It's more of a conclusion than an assumption. Therefore, the option is "]]
