statement because it\'s saying they can, but the statement says they can\'t. So C is probably false.\n\nOption D: People who read all the books must be the most diligent. This is the opposite of what\'s stated. The original says the most diligent can\'t read all, so it doesn\'t imply that only the most diligent can. In fact, maybe someone else is more diligent but still can\'t read all. So D is likely false.\n\nSo, the logical inferences are A and B. But since the question asks for a logical inference, both could be correct, but in multiple-choice settings, usually, the strongest statement is preferred. A is a direct restatement, while B uses "may not," which is a bit softer. However, in logical terms, both are inferences, but A is more precise.\n\nWait, but in logic, if the statement is "Even the most diligent cannot read all," then it\'s a universal statement. So, the inference would be that for all people, if they are the most diligent, they cannot read all. So, A is a specific case of that, making it a valid inference. B is also a valid inference because it\'s a possibility, but A is more direct. Therefore, the option is ']]
