ithout cutting programs. So, the faculty's strike leads to program cuts.\n\nBut wait, the faculty's strike is only if salaries aren't increased. So, the argument is saying that the faculty will strike unless salaries are increased, which implies that if salaries are increased, the faculty won't strike. But the school board's only way to increase salaries is by cutting programs. So, if they cut programs, salaries go up, which would prevent the faculty from striking. But the argument concludes that the faculty will strike, leading to program cuts. Hmm, that seems a bit circular.\n\nWait, maybe I'm getting it wrong. Let me rephrase. The faculty will go on strike unless salaries are increased. So, the condition is: If salaries are not increased, then the faculty will strike. Symbolically, that's ¬Salary → Strike.\n\nBut the school board president knows that to increase salaries, they have to cut programs. So, increasing salaries (Salary) requires cutting programs (Cut). Symbolically, Salary → Cut.\n\nThe conclusion is that some programs will be cut, so Cut.\n\nSo, the argument is: If the faculty goes on strike, then unless salaries are increased, they will. But the school board can only increase salaries by cutting programs. So, the argument is trying to link the faculty's strike to the cutting of programs.\n\nWait, but the argument as presented says that the faculty will go on strike unless salaries are increased, and the school board can only increase salaries by cutting programs. Therefore, the conclusion is that the faculty will go on strike, leading to program cuts.\n\nBut for that to be the case, the school board must be able to increase salaries. Because if they can't, then they can't cut programs to increase salaries, so the faculty won't strike. So, the argument assumes that the school board can indeed increase salaries, which would require cutting programs.\n\nLooking at the options, let's see:.\n\nA) The school board will refuse to increase the faculty's salary. If this were true, then the faculty wouldn't strike, so the conclusion that programs are cut wouldn't follow. So A is the opposite of what's needed.\n\nB) The school board president has the authority to offer the faculty their desired salary increase. This seems relevant because if the president doesn't have the authority, the school board can't increase salaries, so the faculty won't strike, and programs won't be cut. So B is necessary for the conclusion to hold.\n\nC) The faculty won't go on strike. If they don't go on strike, then the conclusion about program cuts doesn't follow. So C is the opposite of what's needed.\n\nD) The faculty won't accept a package of improved benefits. This seems unrelated because the argument is about salary increases, not about benefits. So D doesn't seem relevant. Therefore, the option is "]] 73%|#######3  | 440/600 [08:35<03:32,  1.33s/it]
