id lays eggs on these larvae, and before they become pupae, the larvae are eaten, reducing the pest population. The researchers conclude that applying this to vegetables can reduce the use of pesticides.\n\nThe question is asking which premise must be added to make this conclusion valid. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nI need to think about what the conclusion is. The conclusion is that using transgenic vegetables with increased green fragrance can reduce pesticide use because the natural enemies will take care of the pests. So, the reasoning is that the increased fragrance attracts predators, which then eat the pests before they can harm the crops.\n\nNow, looking at the premises in the context, they mention that when the leaves are injured, the fragrance increases, which lures natural enemies. They also mention that the transgenic method introduces the gene, so the plant produces more of the aroma enzyme, leading to more fragrance. The parasitoid eats the larvae, which are the pests, so the natural enemies are effectively used.\n\nBut to make the conclusion valid, we need to ensure that the increased fragrance actually works as intended. So, the researcher's conclusion is that this method will reduce pesticide use. Therefore, the premise must link the increased fragrance to the effectiveness of the natural enemies.\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) Current pesticides are mainly used for eliminating pests. This might be relevant because if most pesticides are used for this purpose, then reducing their use would be significant. But does this directly support the conclusion? Maybe not directly, because the conclusion is about the effectiveness of natural enemies, not just the type of pesticides used.\n\nB) Fragrance of transgenic leaves will be enhanced after injury. This seems relevant because the context mentions that when leaves are injured, the fragrance increases. So, if the transgenic leaves are modified to produce more fragrance when injured, that supports the 