 options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break down the context step by step. They used existing species' temperature tolerances. They looked at fossils from the same place and same period. They matched the species to determine the max summer temperature.\n\nSo, the key point here is that they used the known temperature ranges of the current species to infer past temperatures. That means they assumed that the temperature tolerances of these beetles haven't changed over time. Because if the tolerances had changed, you couldn't use the current species' tolerances to determine past temperatures accurately.\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) The temperature tolerances of the beetle species did not change significantly during the 22,000-year period.\n\nB) Beetles can tolerate warm weather better than cold weather.\n\nC) The process of dating is more accurate for beetles than for other organisms.\n\nD) Fossils of different species found in the same place belonged to different periods.\n\nSo, the researchers assumed that the temperature tolerances of the beetles they found (which are still existing species) are the same as they were 22,000 years ago. That's exactly what option A states. .\n\nOption B is about the beetle's preference for warm weather, but the context doesn't mention anything about their preference; it's more about using their tolerances to determine temperatures.\n\nOption C talks about the accuracy of dating, but the context doesn't provide any information about the reliability of the dating method itself. It's more about using the species' tolerances.\n\nOption D suggests that different species are from different periods, but the context actually uses the same period for different species found in the same place, which supports the idea that their tolerances are consistent over time. Therefore, the option is "]]
