uld be more than boys in LA. So, I'm not sure if C must be true.\n\nOption D: The total number of boys in science class is more than that of girls in liberal arts class.\n\nThis is comparing boys in S to girls in LA. Again, since there are more boys overall and more science classes, it's possible that the number of boys in S is more than the number of girls in LA. But I'm not sure because it depends on how the students are distributed. Maybe girls are concentrated in LA, and boys in S, but it's not necessarily the case. So, I'm not sure if D must be true.\n\nWait, maybe I should approach this differently. Let's consider the total numbers.\n\nLet me denote:.\n\n- Let B be the total number of boys.\n- Let G be the total number of girls.\n- Let S be the number of science classes.\n- Let LA be the number of liberal arts classes.\n\nGiven that B > G and S > LA.\n\nNow, the total number of students in science classes would be S * average students per class, but since we don't know the exact numbers, maybe it's better to think in terms of ratios.\n\nBut perhaps a better approach is to consider that since there are more science classes, and more boys overall, it's likely that in each science class, there are more boys than girls. Similarly, in LA classes, maybe there are more girls than boys, but that's not necessarily the case.\n\nWait, but the question is about which must be correct. So, let's think about whether any of these options must be true based on the given information.\n\nOption B says that in each science class, boys outnumber girls. But the given information doesn't specify that each science class has more boys than girls. It just says that overall, there are more boys and more science classes. So, it's possible that in some science classes, girls are more than boys, but overall, the total boys in all science classes exceed the total girls.\n\nSimilarly, Option D compares the total boys in science to the total girls in LA. Since B > G and S > LA, it's possible 