er has to be reheated after cooling. So overall, the manager thinks it's a bad idea because it won't bring any profit.\n\nThe supervisor agrees with the overall conclusion but disagrees about one point. The supervisor mentions that the latest closed furnaces are extremely fuel-efficient.\n\nNow, the question is asking which point the supervisor is disagreeing with. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break down each option:.\n\nA) Whether the new process releases less sulfur dioxide. The manager says yes, the new process would reduce emissions. The supervisor doesn't comment on this, so maybe this isn't the point of disagreement.\n\nB) Whether the new process should be adopted. The manager suggests it's not worth it because of costs, but the supervisor agrees with the conclusion, so maybe this isn't the point either.\n\nC) Whether the new process is more expensive to run. The manager says yes, it's more expensive because of reheating. The supervisor's point is about the efficiency of closed furnaces, which relates to running costs. So maybe this is the point.\n\nD) Whether closed furnaces are more fuel-efficient. The supervisor says they are, which directly contradicts the manager's concern about higher running costs. So this must be the point of disagreement.\n\nWait, but the manager's concern was that the new process is more expensive to run, which would increase costs. The supervisor points out that the closed furnaces are fuel-efficient, which would actually reduce running costs. So the supervisor is disagreeing with the manager's assessment of running costs because the new equipment is more efficient than previously thought.\n\nSo the point of disagreement is about the fuel efficiency of the closed furnaces, which affects the running costs. Therefore, the option is "]]
