uan Dynasty inherited the policy of opening up from the Tang and Song dynasties, which means they were actively involved in overseas trade and had strong ties with many countries. It mentions that there are more than 200 countries related to the Yuan Dynasty, though not all were recorded in previous records. So, this suggests that the Yuan Dynasty had extensive contact with many overseas countries.\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) There are far more overseas countries in contact with the yuan dynasty than with the previous dynasties. The context mentions that the Yuan had contact with over 200 countries, which is more than previous dynasties. So this seems consistent.\n\nB) The western expedition of the yuan dynasty reached Africa and drew a map accordingly. The context talks about the Great Triangle of Southern Africa appearing in the Ming Dynasty and Korea's map, which was based on Yuan maps. It doesn't explicitly mention that the Yuan dynasty sent western expeditions to Africa. So I'm not sure if this is correct.\n\nC) The yuan dynasty was the most developed period of maritime traffic in China. The context doesn't provide information about the level of maritime traffic during the Yuan Dynasty specifically. It mentions that the Yuan inherited the open policy, implying they were active, but not necessarily the most developed.\n\nD) The most prosperous overseas trade in China in history was the Yuan dynasty. The context says the Yuan had extensive trade and contact, but it doesn't state that it was the most prosperous. It might have been, but the context doesn't explicitly confirm it. Therefore, the option is "]]
