ting mice eat normally increases the likelihood of leukemia. Hmm, wait. The context says that the mice were exposed to radiation, so the exposure is a given. The difference is in their diet. The group with normal food had 55 cases, and the limited food had 3. So, actually, giving normal food increased leukemia cases. So A might not be correct because it's not about exposure; it's about the food. Plus, the exposure is the same for both groups.\n\nOption B claims that leukemia isn't related to radiation exposure. But the data shows that the group exposed to radiation had higher leukemia rates, especially in the normal food group. So B is definitely wrong because it directly contradicts the results.\n\nOption C states that controlling the amount of food reduces the likelihood of leukemia from radiation. That makes sense because the limited food group had fewer cases (3) compared to the normal food group (55). So controlling the food intake seems to have a protective effect against leukemia in this context. So C looks correct.\n\nOption D says that mice exposed to radiation will definitely develop leukemia. But in the study, only 55 out of 200 developed it, which is a high number but not all. So D is incorrect because it's not a certainty; it's just a higher risk. Therefore, the option is "]]
