y isn't comparable. That's a valid point. If the company's service isn't as good, customers might be less likely to buy it even if it's energy-efficient. But again, the main argument is about sales due to energy use, so this might be a challenge but not the strongest.\n\nC) Service life is lower. If the product doesn't last as long, that's a problem. People might buy it once but then have to buy another, which doesn't directly affect initial sales. It's more about longevity, not market share.\n\nD) Power consumption isn't the main concern. Oh, this seems relevant. If consumers don't prioritize low power use, then even though the product is energy-efficient, it might not appeal to them enough to drive higher sales. This directly challenges the assumption that lower power consumption translates to higher sales. Therefore, the option is "]]
