ng Anthropology Department. However, despite this, the university is still going to fund the new department. .\n\nSo, the question is asking which statement logically follows from these statements. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me break this down. The key points are:.\n\n1. For a new department to be funded, it must not have fewer than 50 hires per year (so at least 50) and it must not duplicate more than 25% of an existing department's material.\n\n2. The proposed Area Studies Department does duplicate more than 25% of Anthropology, which is an existing department.\n\n3. However, the university is still funding the new department. That means even though it duplicates more than 25%, it's still being funded. So, the first condition (fewer than 50 hires) must not be met. Therefore, the field must have at least 50 people available for hire.\n\nNow, looking at the options:.\n\nA) If the department didn't duplicate more than 25% of another department, then it wouldn't be funded. But in our case, it does duplicate more than 25%, so this doesn't directly follow. Also, the funding isn't because of the duplication; it's because of the number of hires. So A might not be correct.\n\nB) It says the department doesn't duplicate more than 25% in any other department except Anthropology. But the context only mentions duplication with Anthropology. There's no info about other departments, so B isn't necessarily true.\n\nC) The field has fewer than 50 hires. But the opposite is true because the department is being funded, so it must have at least 50. So C is incorrect.\n\nD) The field has at least 50 hires. This aligns with what we concluded earlier because the duplication caused a condition to fail, but funding happened, so the other condition (hires) must be met. Therefore, the option is "]]
