ariation on a theme.\nHmm, the context says that every idea is a variation of previous themes, so it's likely that great discoverers have done this. But does it mean all of them have? Maybe some might have been exceptions, but the passage doesn't explicitly say that all great discoverers have done this. It just states that every idea is a variation. So maybe A is not necessarily true.\n\nOption B: Careful analysis of a specific variation can reveal previous themes of which it is a variation.\nThe context says that every idea is a variation, so analyzing it should show the previous themes. This seems directly supported by the passage. So B must be true.\n\nOption C: Some new scientific discoveries do not represent, on a deep level, a variation on previous themes.\nWait, the context says that every new idea is a variation. So this option is saying the opposite. If every new idea is a variation, then it's impossible for some to not be. So C must be false, meaning it's the exception we're looking for.\n\nOption D: No scientific idea is entirely independent of all other ideas.\nThe context mentions that every idea is built upon previous ones, so none are entirely independent. So D must be true.\n\nSo, putting it together, the question is asking which one does NOT have to be true. From my analysis, C is the one that doesn't have to be true because the passage states that every idea is a variation, so there can't be any that aren't. Therefore, the option is "]]
