messages:  [['Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format "\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D".\n####\n# Context:\nWhen you are about to graduate, a certain class will select outstanding graduates, and discussions are ongoing within the class. Class leader: Either Li Xue is rated as an outstanding graduate or Wang Lei is rated as an outstanding graduate. Youth League Party Secretary: I disagree.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following accurately expresses the opinion of the party secretary?\n# Options:\nA) Both li xue and wang lei were rated as outstanding graduates\nB) Neither li xue nor wang lei can be rated as outstanding graduates\nC) Either li xue and wang lei are both rated as outstanding graduates, or neither li xue nor wang lei can be rated as outstanding graduates\nD) Li xue was rated as an outstanding graduate, wang lei cannot be rated as an outstanding graduate\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:', '.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says that when the class is about to graduate, they\'re selecting outstanding graduates. There are two people involved: Li Xue and Wang Lei. The class leader says that either Li Xue is rated as an outstanding graduate or Wang Lei is rated as an outstanding graduate. Then the Youth League Party Secretary disagrees with that statement.\n\nThe question is asking which option accurately expresses the opinion of the party secretary. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nFirst, I need to understand what the class leader is saying. The class leader is stating that at least one of the two, Li Xue or Wang Lei, will be rated as an outstanding graduate. In logical terms, this is an "inclusive OR," meaning either one is true, or both are true. So, the class leader\'s statement can be represented as L ∨ W, where L is Li Xue being outstanding and W is Wang Lei being outstanding.\n\nNow, the Youth League Party Secretary disagrees with this statement. So, the party secretary\'s opinion is the opposite of what the class leader is saying. The opposite of an inclusive OR is an inclusive AND of the negations. In other words, the party secretary is saying that neither Li Xue nor Wang Lei is rated as an outstanding graduate. Symbolically, this would be ¬L ∧ ¬W.\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) Both Li Xue and Wang Lei were rated as outstanding graduates. This is L ∧ W, which is not the negation of the class leader\'s statement. The class leader\'s statement is L ∨ W, so the negation would be ¬L ∧ ¬W, which is not the same as both being true. So A is not correct.\n\nB) Neither Li Xue nor Wang Lei can be rated as outstanding graduates. This is ¬L ∧ ¬W, which is exactly the negation of the class leader\'s statement. So B seems correct.\n\nC) Either Li Xue and Wang Lei are both rated as outstanding graduates, or neither Li Xue nor Wang Lei can be rated as outstanding graduates. This is (L ∧ W) ∨ (¬L ∧ ¬W), which is actually the law of excluded middle, meaning either both are true or both are false. But the class leader\'s statement is L ∨ W, so the negation is ¬L ∧ ¬W, not this. So C is not correct.\n\nD) Li Xue was rated as an outstanding graduate, Wang Lei cannot be rated as an outstanding graduate. This is L ∧ ¬W, which is a specific case where only Li Xue is outstanding. But the negation of the class leader\'s statement is that both are not outstanding, not that one is and the other isn\'t. So D is not correct. Therefore, the option is ']]
